    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="3">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:QuadrDed">Quadr Ded</ref> 3</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Quadr Ded 3" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="529" ed="#liebermann"/>Uereor tamen, ne dum tuæ circa nos beneuolentiæ<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#QuadrDed_3.lnote.oe.1"/> gratios[u]s esse desidero, ridicul[u]s<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#QuadrDed_3.lnote.oe.2"/> inueniar, ut tuam quoque spectet iniuriam<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="d" target="#QuadrDed_3.lnote.oe.d"/>.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 529 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_3.lnote.oe.d" n="d" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">529</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl. u.</hi> 24</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">cf. below</hi> 24</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_3.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">529</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">beniu. gratiosos <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">beniu. gratiosos <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_3.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">529</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ridiculos <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ridiculos <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="QuadrDed_3.lnote.oe.a" n="a" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">311</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Dedikat steht also sozial über dem Verfasser, ist aber, weil ‘du’ und nicht ‘Vater’ angeredet, nicht Bischof.—<hi rend="italic">Vgl</hi>. Arg. 21</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The dedicatee therefore stands socially above the author, but, since he is addressed as ‘thou’ and not as ‘father’, is not a bishop.—<hi rend="italic">Cf</hi>. Arg. 21</seg></note>
      </div>
